From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Jaime Dias <jaimejosediasnt@gmail.com>
Date: Tue, 19 Aug 2026 15:40:00 -0300
Subject: [PATCH 41/41] mungwall: when the header is gone, say what is there
 and who broke it

A `mungwall` boot on the Pi 3 moved ISSUE-0037's failure two thirds of a
boot earlier and gave it a call chain:

    IntuitionInit -> MakePointerFromPrefs -> MakePointerFromData
      -> NewObjectA (pointerclass) -> AllocSpriteDataA
        -> Graphics BitMapScale -> HIDD BitMapScale -> FreeVec(dstbuf)

    [MungWall] FreeMem(0x020c8830, 72) from FreeMem: no mungwall header
    [Kernel:TLSF] free-list corruption at double free: block=020c8828
        size=3688618968 flags=0x3 prev=dbdbdbdb next=00000044

0xDBDBDBD8 and prev=0xdbdbdbdb are wall fill, so the memory under that
pointer is a mungwall wall -- the header that should sit below it is not
there. `BM__Hidd_BitMap__BitMapScale()` allocates three buffers and frees
the same three pointers, so the pointer is not the bug; the header being
gone is.

That leaves one question -- gone how -- and the existing message cannot
answer it. Three very different situations print the same line: a block
older than mungwall reads as whatever the allocator left behind, a block
whose header was overwritten reads as somebody else's data, and a block
that is all 0xDB is a wall, meaning the pointer never was the start of an
allocation. Only the words tell them apart, so print them.

Then run MungWall_Scan() from the same place. It checks every live
allocation's walls, and a neighbour that overran its own block reports
"Post-wall broken" together with the task and the caller that allocated
it. That caller is the writer this whole exercise is looking for, and
catching it at the first sign of damage rather than at the next unrelated
free is the entire reason for turning mungwall on.

Diagnostic only, and only on a path that has already found damage:
mungwall is a boot argument, so nothing here runs on a normal boot.
diff --git a/rom/exec/mungwall.c b/rom/exec/mungwall.c
index c7f514e45b..a9b304d660 100644
--- a/rom/exec/mungwall.c
+++ b/rom/exec/mungwall.c
@@ -198,10 +198,41 @@ APTR MungWall_Check(APTR memoryBlock, IPTR byteSize, struct TraceLocation *loc,
          */
         if (header->mwh_magicid != MUNGWALL_HEADER_ID)
         {
+            const ULONG *w = (const ULONG *)header;
+            unsigned int i;
+
             bug("[MungWall] FreeMem(0x%p, %lu) from %s: no mungwall header"
                 " (pre-mungwall or foreign allocation) - freeing as-is\n",
                 memoryBlock, (unsigned long)byteSize,
                 loc && loc->function ? loc->function : "<unknown>");
+
+            /*
+             * Say what is there instead. A block allocated before mungwall
+             * came on reads as whatever the allocator left behind; one whose
+             * header was overwritten reads as somebody else's data; one that
+             * is all 0xDB is a wall, which means this pointer is not the
+             * start of an allocation at all. The three are worth telling
+             * apart, and the words are the only thing that tells them apart.
+             */
+            bug("[MungWall] header region at 0x%p:", header);
+            for (i = 0; i < MUNGWALL_BLOCK_SHIFT / sizeof(ULONG); i++)
+            {
+                if ((i & 3) == 0)
+                    bug("\n[MungWall]   +%02x:", (unsigned)(i * sizeof(ULONG)));
+                bug(" %08x", w[i]);
+            }
+            bug("\n");
+
+            /*
+             * Then ask every live allocation whether its walls are intact.
+             * A neighbour that overran its own block reports "Post-wall
+             * broken" with the task and caller that allocated it, which is
+             * the writer this whole exercise is looking for -- and finding it
+             * here, at the first sign of damage, is the point of turning
+             * mungwall on at all.
+             */
+            MungWall_Scan(NULL, loc, SysBase);
+
             return memoryBlock;
         }
 
